: "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c6b8f94f09735fa6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe most deadly types of cancers include <strong>lung, colon, pancreatic cancer, breast, and prostate cancers</strong>. Keep in mind that estimated deaths can vary between males and females and may be affected by age and family history.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c6b8f94f09735fa6\">>>", "published": "April 14, 2015", "siteName": "www.verywellhealth.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"befd5a4ba11a1134\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nAmerican Cancer Society Study Projects 2024 Cancer Incidence and Mortality | CancerNetwork\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"befd5a4ba11a1134\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancernetwork.com/view/american-cancer-society-study-projects-2024-cancer-incidence-and-mortality", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b53dec14f36c5a4e\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFrom the mid-1970s to 2013 to 2019, the 5-year survival rate for all cancers had increased from 49% to 69%. Survival rates were highest among those with <strong>thyroid (99%), prostate (97%), and testis cancer (95%) as well as melanoma (94%).</strong>\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b53dec14f36c5a4e\">>>", "published": "3 weeks ago", "siteName": "www.cancernetwork.com"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775815304694}}
{"type": "message", "id": "323b6f65", "parentId": "66d04585", "timestamp": "2026-04-10T10:01:44.696Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "toolu_bdrk_01VZyHNfb7kq8wG6wAw8bbCu", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"uncommon rare cancers cases per year United States incidence\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 5,\n  \"tookMs\": 972,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"990dfbdab03ba984\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRare Cancers Research Program, Congressionally Directed Medical Research Programs\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"990dfbdab03ba984\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://cdmrp.health.mil/rcrp/default\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"81357a766fb696bb\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA recent report from the American Cancer Society (ACS) found that one in five cancers diagnosed in the United States (U.S.) is a rare cancer, which is defined as an incidence of <strong>fewer than six cases per 100,000 people per year</strong>. By this definition, around 200 forms of rare cancer compose around ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"81357a766fb696bb\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"cdmrp.health.mil\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ed59544246f4ead7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nChecking your browser - reCAPTCHA\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ed59544246f4ead7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39817679/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e070274dbf38b888\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nChecking your browser before accessing pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov · Click here if you are not automatically redirected after 5 seconds\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e070274dbf38b888\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dc385dfd20aa86f7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCancer Statistics - NCI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dc385dfd20aa86f7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/understanding/statistics\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"da0e90d883fb6cff\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe best indicator of progress against cancer is a change in age-adjusted mortality (death) rates, although other measures, such as quality of life, are also important. Incidence is also important, but it is not always straightforward to interpret changes in incidence. A rise in incidence can reflect a real increase in disease occurrence, such as when an increase in exposure to a risk factor causes more cases of cancer.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"da0e90d883fb6cff\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6b6bf3a9d037bcac\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHighlights from 2025 U.S. Cancer Statistics | U.S. Cancer Statistics | CDC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6b6bf3a9d037bcac\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cdc.gov/united-states-cancer-statistics/publications/uscs-highlights.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a08f901b95b122c1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThis online tool shows the numbers ... levels. The U.S. Cancer Statistics Public Use Database includes detailed, de-identified information on more than <strong>39 million</strong> cancer cases diagnosed since 2001. It is a freely available and unparalleled resource for investigating cancer incidence ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a08f901b95b122c1\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"June 11, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \